# Git hooks and shell scripts MUST stay LF in the working tree. A CRLF in the
# shebang makes Git-for-Windows' sh fail with "bad interpreter: .../bash^M",
# silently disabling the hook. eol=lf overrides core.autocrlf on checkout.
.githooks/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Node scripts must stay LF for the SAME reason, and the failure is quieter.
# vite/esbuild strip a shebang by matching `#!...\n`; with CRLF the stray \r
# survives and the module fails to parse — so `npm run test:scripts` died with
# "SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token" and ran ZERO tests, while
# scripts/verify.mjs still invoked it in the web lane.
#
# It only bites on Windows: the blobs are LF, core.autocrlf=true rewrites them
# to CRLF on checkout, and CI on Linux therefore stayed green the whole time. A
# suite that looks like coverage and runs nothing is worse than no suite, and
# nothing failed to signal it. eol=lf overrides core.autocrlf on checkout.
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf

# The Gradle wrapper has no extension, so `*.sh` above never covered it. It also
# needs its EXECUTABLE BIT set in the index (100755) — it was committed 100644,
# which made `./gradlew assembleDebug` die with "Permission denied" (exit 126) as
# the FIRST step of Android CI. That lane therefore never once compiled the
# module: the job failed before Gradle started, on every push since it was added.
# Both halves matter, and the mode is not something .gitattributes can enforce —
# if you ever re-add this file, check `git ls-files -s android/gradlew` says
# 100755.
gradlew text eol=lf

# Migrations must stay LF because tests COMPARE THEIR BYTES. sizing-chart-parity
# re-runs the generator and asserts the committed 00498/00499 match it exactly,
# and authenticity-references asserts 00500 contains a literal "…,\n  false,"
# (the load-bearing `public = false` on a private bucket). The generators emit
# LF, so a CRLF working tree fails all three on a `\r` — while CI on Linux stays
# green, because the blobs are LF and only core.autocrlf=true rewrites them.
#
# Third time this exact shape has bitten (see .sh and .mjs above), and the worst
# variant: `npm run verify` IS the pre-push hook, so on Windows the gate failed
# for a reason that had nothing to do with the change being pushed — which
# teaches --no-verify. Postgres does not care either way.
*.sql text eol=lf

# FOURTH time (US-2429), and the one the three rules above should have implied.
# This repo leans hard on SOURCE-SCAN guard tests: a test reads a .ts file's
# bytes and asserts a structural property that cannot be checked by running the
# code (a tenant scope, a plan gate, a `continue` inside a catch). Those scans
# are written against LF, because that is what the author sees in the blob —
# `indexOf('.from("x")\n    .select(')` and `/catch \(err\)[\s\S]{0,400}continue;/`
# are both real examples that failed here. The first never matches `\r\n` at all;
# the second runs out of window because CRLF adds a byte per line.
#
# So the guard reports that a security property was REMOVED when nothing was
# touched. That is worse than a flaky test: it is a false alarm on exactly the
# assertions you most need to believe, and `npm run verify` is the pre-push hook.
#
# Fixing the scans to tolerate whitespace is still right and US-2429 does that
# too — this rule removes the class, the tolerant patterns survive a checkout
# that predates it. Blobs are already LF, so this changes the working tree only.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf

# FIFTH time, same shape (US-2886). scripts/prd-story.test.mjs asserts the
# backlog files are canonically formatted by re-serializing them and comparing
# the BYTES: re-serializing at two-space indent with a trailing newline must
# give back the file exactly.
# JSON.stringify emits LF, so on a CRLF working tree the comparison fails by
# exactly one byte per line -- 42,081 of them on prd.archive.json -- and reports
# the archive as malformed when nothing is wrong with it. CI on Linux stays
# green, so `npm run verify` fails only on the machine that runs it as the
# pre-push hook. The writers (scripts/prd-story.mjs and friends) all emit LF.
prd.json text eol=lf
prd.archive.json text eol=lf
prd-seo.json text eol=lf
prd-connector.json text eol=lf
